{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":49743,"num_turns":2,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"ed29a742-b07d-5d28-a8f2-144a5ac49ed5","total_cost_usd":0.432984,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9690,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":4695,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9690,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4695,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9690,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9690},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":4309,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004414,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":4695,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9690,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.42857,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\":\"actor:dependence-free-batch-j2-reviewer-fable-46\",\"reviews\":[{\"case_id\":\"case:0746e42a0faa024f7f29\",\"verdict\":\"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope\",\"bounded_statement\":null,\"root_cause\":null,\"issue_class\":null,\"evidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"All 24 crab_id values in data/input.csv are distinct (MC01 through MC24, one row each), so no independent unit contributes more than one row to the binomial test.\",\"observed_value\":{\"n_rows\":24,\"n_distinct_crab_id\":24},\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":1,\"end_line\":2,\"quoted_text\":\"crab_id,sex,carapace_width_mm,arm_choice\\nMC01,F,38.4,tidal\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":25,\"end_line\":25,\"quoted_text\":\"MC24,M,40.4,tidal\"}]},{\"description\":\"The data description states each crab was tested exactly once and never retested, and that each crab_id appears on exactly one row, so rows are one-trial-per-independent-unit by design.\",\"observed_value\":\"one row per crab, no retesting\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":7,\"end_line\":9,\"quoted_text\":\"The arm the crab entered first was recorded, the crab\\nwas removed, and it was never tested again. Every crab therefore appears on\\nexactly one line of the file, and no line summarises more than one crab.\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":11,\"end_line\":13,\"quoted_text\":\"One row is: one juvenile mud crab that completed a single Y-maze arm-choice trial\\nIndependent unit column: crab_id\\nOne trial is: one row\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow enforces uniqueness of crab_id at runtime, raising a ValueError if any crab code appears on more than one row, before any statistics are computed.\",\"observed_value\":\"duplicate-guard present in read_trials\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":28,\"end_line\":30,\"quoted_text\":\"codes = [row[\\\"crab_id\\\"].strip() for row in rows]\\n    if len(set(codes)) != len(codes):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"each crab_id must appear on exactly one row\\\")\"}]},{\"description\":\"The reported counts reproduce from the raw rows: 19 tidal and 5 control choices among 24 crabs, and the exact two-sided binomial test against p = 0.5 gives the reported p = 0.006611; the selected result is computed on one independent Bernoulli outcome per crab.\",\"observed_value\":{\"n_tidal\":19,\"n_control\":5,\"n_units\":24,\"reported_p\":0.006611},\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":12,\"end_line\":13,\"quoted_text\":\"- Crabs tested (independent units): 24\\n- Rows in data file: 24\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":34,\"end_line\":34,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test, 19/24 crabs chose the tidal-cue arm (proportion 0.7917) against the chance expectation of 0.5: p = 0.006611; the preference is significant at alpha = 0.05.\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The analysis derives its sample size from a raw row count (n_units = len(rows)), which would silently inflate n if any crab occupied multiple rows; however, this pathway is closed here because read_trials rejects any input with a repeated crab_id and the supplied file contains no duplicates, so this does not demonstrate the issue.\",\"observed_value\":\"n taken from row count but guarded by uniqueness check\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":39,\"end_line\":39,\"quoted_text\":\"    n_units = len(rows)\"},{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":29,\"end_line\":30,\"quoted_text\":\"if len(set(codes)) != len(codes):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"each crab_id must appear on exactly one row\\\")\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I actively tried to demonstrate the issue by searching for any way a single independent unit could enter the row-independent binomial test more than once: I enumerated all 24 crab_id values in data/input.csv and confirmed they are pairwise distinct (MC01-MC24, each once); I checked the data description for any hint of retesting, split trials, or multi-crab rows and found the opposite is explicitly stated; I traced the workflow to confirm the test statistic (n_tidal out of n_units) counts each row exactly once with no aggregation ambiguity, and that a duplicate crab_id would abort execution before the test runs. I also recomputed the tallies (19/24 tidal, 12F/12M, subgroup splits 8/12 and 11/12) and confirmed they match the report, ruling out hidden double-counting. No route to repeated-unit entry survived these checks.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-j2-reviewer-fable-46","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:0746e42a0faa024f7f29","verdict":"no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope","bounded_statement":null,"root_cause":null,"issue_class":null,"evidence_atoms":[{"description":"All 24 crab_id values in data/input.csv are distinct (MC01 through MC24, one row each), so no independent unit contributes more than one row to the binomial test.","observed_value":{"n_rows":24,"n_distinct_crab_id":24},"source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":1,"end_line":2,"quoted_text":"crab_id,sex,carapace_width_mm,arm_choice\nMC01,F,38.4,tidal"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":25,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"MC24,M,40.4,tidal"}]},{"description":"The data description states each crab was tested exactly once and never retested, and that each crab_id appears on exactly one row, so rows are one-trial-per-independent-unit by design.","observed_value":"one row per crab, no retesting","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":7,"end_line":9,"quoted_text":"The arm the crab entered first was recorded, the crab\nwas removed, and it was never tested again. Every crab therefore appears on\nexactly one line of the file, and no line summarises more than one crab."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":11,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"One row is: one juvenile mud crab that completed a single Y-maze arm-choice trial\nIndependent unit column: crab_id\nOne trial is: one row"}]},{"description":"The workflow enforces uniqueness of crab_id at runtime, raising a ValueError if any crab code appears on more than one row, before any statistics are computed.","observed_value":"duplicate-guard present in read_trials","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":28,"end_line":30,"quoted_text":"codes = [row[\"crab_id\"].strip() for row in rows]\n    if len(set(codes)) != len(codes):\n        raise ValueError(\"each crab_id must appear on exactly one row\")"}]},{"description":"The reported counts reproduce from the raw rows: 19 tidal and 5 control choices among 24 crabs, and the exact two-sided binomial test against p = 0.5 gives the reported p = 0.006611; the selected result is computed on one independent Bernoulli outcome per crab.","observed_value":{"n_tidal":19,"n_control":5,"n_units":24,"reported_p":0.006611},"source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":12,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"- Crabs tested (independent units): 24\n- Rows in data file: 24"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":34,"end_line":34,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test, 19/24 crabs chose the tidal-cue arm (proportion 0.7917) against the chance expectation of 0.5: p = 0.006611; the preference is significant at alpha = 0.05."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The analysis derives its sample size from a raw row count (n_units = len(rows)), which would silently inflate n if any crab occupied multiple rows; however, this pathway is closed here because read_trials rejects any input with a repeated crab_id and the supplied file contains no duplicates, so this does not demonstrate the issue.","observed_value":"n taken from row count but guarded by uniqueness check","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":39,"end_line":39,"quoted_text":"    n_units = len(rows)"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":29,"end_line":30,"quoted_text":"if len(set(codes)) != len(codes):\n        raise ValueError(\"each crab_id must appear on exactly one row\")"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I actively tried to demonstrate the issue by searching for any way a single independent unit could enter the row-independent binomial test more than once: I enumerated all 24 crab_id values in data/input.csv and confirmed they are pairwise distinct (MC01-MC24, each once); I checked the data description for any hint of retesting, split trials, or multi-crab rows and found the opposite is explicitly stated; I traced the workflow to confirm the test statistic (n_tidal out of n_units) counts each row exactly once with no aggregation ambiguity, and that a duplicate crab_id would abort execution before the test runs. I also recomputed the tallies (19/24 tidal, 12F/12M, subgroup splits 8/12 and 11/12) and confirmed they match the report, ruling out hidden double-counting. No route to repeated-unit entry survived these checks.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":24167,"ttft_stream_ms":1297,"time_to_request_ms":11,"type":"result","duration_ms":48532,"uuid":"4b46010f-52de-4d45-bc25-5764cebb6faf"}
